                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/GuillouEquivariantHomotopyAndCohomology.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
          %p Textbooks and lecture notes:
          %ul
            %li#tomDieck79
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tammo+tom+Dieck"}> Tammo tom Dieck
                , section 7 of
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Transformation+Groups+and+Representation+Theory"} Transformation Groups and Representation Theory
            %li#May96
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+May"} Peter May
                et al.,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Equivariant homotopy and cohomology theory
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://bookstore.ams.org/cbms-91"} ISBN:978-0-8218-0319-6
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.math.uchicago.edu/~may/BOOKS/alaska.pdf"} pdf
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/MayEtAlEquivariant96.pdf"} pdf
            %li
              %p
                Matvei Libine,
                %em Lecture Notes on Equivariant Cohomology
                (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0709.3615"}> arXiv
                )
            %li
              %p
                Sébastien Racanière,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Lecture on Equivariant Cohomology
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/RacaniereEquivariant04.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
          %p For a brief modern survey see also the first three sections of
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+Hill"}> Michael Hill
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+Hopkins"} Michael Hopkins
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Douglas+Ravenel"} Douglas Ravenel
                %em The Arf-Kervaire problem in algebraic topology: Sketch of the proof
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/HHRKervaire.pdf", :title => "pdf"}> pdf
                )
              %p
                (with an eye towards application to the
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Arf-Kervaire+invariant+problem"} Arf-Kervaire invariant problem
            %li
              %p
                blog on
                %a{:href => "http://www.aimath.org/wiki/localization/index.php/Main_Page"} Localization techniques in Equivariant Cohomology
          %p
            Discussion of equivariant versions of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+cohomology"} differential cohomology
            is in
          %ul
            %li
              Andreas Kübel,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andreas+Thom"} Andreas Thom
              = succeed "," do
                %em Equivariant Differential Cohomology
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1510.06392"} arXiv:1510.06392
          %p
            See also at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivariant+de+Rham+cohomology"} equivariant de Rham cohomology
          %h3#InComplexOrientedGeneralizedCohomologyTheory In complex oriented generalized cohomology theory
          %p
            Equivariant
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+oriented+cohomology+theory"} complex oriented cohomology theory
            is discussed in the following articles.
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+Hopkins"}> Michael Hopkins
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Nicholas+Kuhn"} Nicholas Kuhn
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Douglas+Ravenel"} Douglas Ravenel
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Generalized group characters and complex oriented cohomology theories
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.ams.org/journals/jams/2000-13-03/S0894-0347-00-00332-5/"} publisher
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.math.rochester.edu/people/faculty/doug/mypapers/hkr.pdf"} pdf
              %p
                (This deals with “naive” Borel-equivariant complex oriented cohomology, but discusses general
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/character"} character
                expressions and explicit formulas for equivariant
                = succeed "-cohomology.)" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Morava+K-theory"} K(n)
          %p
            Specifically equivariant
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+cobordism+cohomology"} complex cobordism cohomology
            is discussed in
          %ul
            %li#tomDiek70
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tammo+tom+Dieck"}> Tammo tom Dieck
                ,
                %em
                  Bordism of
                  = succeed "-manifolds" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi G
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            %li#Abrams13a
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/William+Abram"}> William Abram
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Equivariant complex cobordism
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "http://deepblue.lib.umich.edu/handle/2027.42/99796"} web
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://deepblue.lib.umich.edu/bitstream/handle/2027.42/99796/abramwc_1.pdf?sequence=1"} pdf
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/William+Abram"}> William Abram
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Igor+Kriz"} Igor Kriz
                %em The equivariant complex cobordism ring of a finite abelian group
                (
                %a{:href => "http://www.math.lsa.umich.edu/~ikriz/cobordism13022-1.pdf"}> pdf
                )
          %p
            The following articles discuss
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivariant+formal+group+laws"} equivariant formal group laws
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Greenlees"}> John Greenlees
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Equivariant formal group laws and complex oriented cohomology theories
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://projecteuclid.org/euclid.hha/1139840255"} EUCLID
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/William+Abram"}> William Abram
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em On the equivariant formal group law of the equivariant complex cobordism ring
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1309.0722"} arXiv:1309.0722
              %p
                (also
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a{:href => "#Abrams13a"} Abrams 13a, section III
          %p
            See also the references at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivariant+elliptic+cohomology"} equivariant elliptic cohomology
          %div
            %h3#traditional_orbifold_cohomology Traditional orbifold cohomology
            %p
              Traditionally, the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology"} cohomology
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orbifolds"} orbifolds
              has, by and large, been taken to be simply the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinary+cohomology"} ordinary cohomology
              of (the plain
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type"} homotopy type
              of) the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+realization+of+simplicial+topological+spaces"} geometric realization
              of the
              = succeed "/" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+groupoid"} topological
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+groupoid"} Lie groupoid
              corresponding to the orbifold.
            %p
              For the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/global+quotient+orbifold"} global quotient orbifold
              of a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+G-space"} G-space
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinary+cohomology"} ordinary cohomology
              of (the bare
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type"} homotopy type
              of) the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Borel+construction"} Borel construction
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mspace{:width => "negativethinmathspace"}
                      %mo ⫽
                      %mspace{:width => "negativethinmathspace"}
                      %mi G
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mo ≃
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi X
                      %msub
                        %mo ×
                        %mi G
                      %mi E
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \!\sslash\! G \;\simeq\; X \times_G E G
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Borel+cohomology"} Borel cohomology
              (as opposed to finer versions of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivariant+cohomology"} equivariant cohomology
              such as
              = succeed ")." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bredon+cohomology"} Bredon cohomology
            %p
              A dedicated account of this Borel cohomology of orbifolds, in the generality of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/twisted+cohomology"} twisted cohomology
              (i.e. with
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+coefficients"} local coefficients
            %ul
              %li#MoerdijkPronk99
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ieke+Moerdijk"}> Ieke Moerdijk
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dorette+Pronk"} Dorette Pronk
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Simplicial cohomology of orbifolds
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/S0019-3577(99)80021-4"} doi:10.1016/S0019-3577(99)80021-4
            %p
              Moreover, since the orbifold’s
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isotropy+groups"} isotropy groups
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi G
                      %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G_x
              are, by definition,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+groups"} finite groups
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classifying+spaces"} classifying spaces
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo *
                    %mspace{:width => "negativethinmathspace"}
                    %mo ⫽
                    %mspace{:width => "negativethinmathspace"}
                    %mi G
                    %mo ≃
                    %mi B
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ast \!\sslash\! G \simeq B G
              have purely
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/torsion+subgroup"} torsion
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integral+cohomology"} integral cohomology
              in
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/positive+number"} positive
              degrees, and hence become indistinguishable from the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/point"} point
              in
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rational+cohomology"} rational cohomology
              (and more generally whenever their
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/order+of+a+group"} order
              is
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unit"} invertible
              in the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coefficient"} coefficient
              = succeed ")." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring"} ring
            %p
              Therefore, in the special case of
              = succeed "/" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rational+cohomology"} rational
              = succeed "/" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+cohomology"} real
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+cohomology"} complex
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coefficients"} coefficients
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type"} homotopy type
              of) the naive quotient underlying an orbifold. For global quotient orbifolds this is the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+quotient+space"} topological quotient space
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X/G
            %p
              In this form, as an invariant of just
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X/G
              = succeed "/" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+cohomology"} real
              = succeed "/" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+cohomology"} complex
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/de+Rham+cohomology"} de Rham cohomology
              of orbifolds was originally introduced in
            %ul
              %li#Satake56
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ichiro+Satake"}> Ichiro Satake
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em On a generalisation of the notion of manifold
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.42.6.359"} doi:10.1073/pnas.42.6.359
            %p following analogous constructions in
            %ul
              %li
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Walter+Lewis+Baily"}> Walter Lewis Baily
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em On the quotient of an analytic manifold by a group of analytic homeomorphisms
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.40.9.804"} doi:10.1073/pnas.40.9.804
            %p
              Since this traditional
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rational+cohomology"} rational cohomology
              of orbifolds does, hence, not actually reflect the specific nature of orbifolds, a proposal for a finer notion of orbifold cohomology was famously introduced (motivated from orbifolds as
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/target+spaces"} target spaces
              in
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2d+CFTs"} 2d CFTs
            %ul
              %li#ChenRuan00
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Weimin+Chen"}> Weimin Chen
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yongbin+Ruan"} Yongbin Ruan
                = succeed "," do
                  %em A New Cohomology Theory for Orbifold
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0004129"} arXiv:math/0004129
            %p
              However,
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chen-Ruan+cohomology"} Chen-Ruan cohomology
              of an orbifold
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒳
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{X}
              turns out to be just Borel cohomology with rational coefficients, hence is just Satake’s coarse cohomology – but applied to the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inertia+orbifold"} inertia orbifold
              of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒳
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{X}
            %ul
              %li#Clader14
                Emily Clader,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Orbifolds and orbifold cohomology
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://www-personal.umich.edu/~eclader/OctLect1.pdf"} pdf
            %p
              Hence
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chen-Ruan+cohomology"} Chen-Ruan cohomology
              of a global quotient orbifold is equivalently the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rational+cohomology"} rational cohomology
              (
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+cohomology"}> real cohomology
              ,
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+cohomology"} complex cohomology
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+quotient+space"} topological quotient space
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi AutMor
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mspace{:width => "negativethinmathspace"}
                    %mo ⫽
                    %mspace{:width => "negativethinmathspace"}
                    %mi G
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} AutMor(X\!\sslash\!G)/G
              of the space of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/automorphisms"} automorphisms
              in the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+groupoid"} action groupoid
              by the
              = succeed "-" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conjugation"} conjugation
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action"} action
            %p On the other hand, it was observed in (see p. 18)
            %ul
              %li#Moerdijk02
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ieke+Moerdijk"}> Ieke Moerdijk
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Orbifolds as Groupoids: an Introduction
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alejandro+Adem"} Alejandro Adem
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jack+Morava"} Jack Morava
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yongbin+Ruan"} Yongbin Ruan
                (eds.)
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Orbifolds+in+Mathematics+and+Physics"} Orbifolds in Mathematics and Physics
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math.DG/0203100"} arXiv:math.DG/0203100
            %p
              that for global quotient orbifolds Chen-Ruan cohomology indeed is equivalent to a
              = succeed "-equivariant" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bredon+cohomology"} Bredon cohomology
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              – for one specific choice of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivariant+cohomology"} equivariant
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coefficient"} coefficient
              system (
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+sheaf"} abelian sheaf
              on the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orbit+category"} orbit category
              of
              = succeed ")," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi H
                      %mo ↦
                      %mi ClassFunctions
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi H
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G/H \mapsto ClassFunctions(H)
            %p
              Or rather,
              %a{:href => "#Moerdijk02"} Moerdijk 02, p. 18
              observes that the Chen-Ruan cohomology of a global quotient orbifold is equivalently the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+sheaf+cohomology"} abelian sheaf cohomology
              of the naive quotient space
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X/G
              with
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coefficients"} coefficients
              in the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+sheaf"} abelian sheaf
              whose
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stalk"} stalk
              at
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [x] \in X/G
              is the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring"} ring
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/class+functions"} class functions
              of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isotropy+group"} isotropy group
              at
              = succeed ";" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            %ul
              %li#Honkasalu90
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hannu+Honkasalo"}> Hannu Honkasalo
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Equivariant Alexander-Spanier cohomology for actions of compact Lie groups
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://www.jstor.org/stable/24492569"} jstor:24492569
            %p
              for the followup statement that the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+sheaf+cohomology"} abelian sheaf cohomology
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X/G
              with coefficient sheaf
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %munder
                      %mi A
                      %mo ̲
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \underline{A}
              being “
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+constant+sheaf"} locally constant
              except for dependence on
              = succeed "\u201D" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isotropy+groups"} isotropy groups
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bredon+cohomology"} Bredon cohomology
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              with coefficients in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi H
                    %mo ↦
                    %msub
                      %munder
                        %mi A
                        %mo ̲
                      %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G/H \mapsto \underline{A}_x
              for
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi Isotr
                        %mi x
                      %mo =
                      %mi H
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Isotr_x = H
            %ul
              %li#Honkasalo88
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hannu+Honkasalo"}> Hannu Honkasalo
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Equivariant Alexander-Spanier cohomology
                %strong 63
                (1988) 179-195 [
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.7146/math.scand.a-12232"}> doi:10.7146/math.scand.a-12232
                ]
            %p See also Section 4.3 of
            %ul
              %li#SzaboValentino07
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Richard+Szabo"}> Richard Szabo
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alessandro+Valentino"} Alessandro Valentino
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Ramond-Ramond Fields, Fractional Branes and Orbifold Differential K-Theory
                %strong 294
                (2010) 647-702 [
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/s00220-009-0975-1"}> doi:10.1007/s00220-009-0975-1
                ,
                = succeed "]" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/0710.2773"} arXiv:0710.2773
            %p In summary:
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  Traditional orbifold cohomology theory is
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Borel+cohomology"} Borel cohomology
                  of underlying
                  = succeed "-spaces," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Borel+construction"} Borel construction
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rational+cohomology"} rationally
                  further to the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rational+cohomology"} rational cohomology
                  of underlying naive
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quotient+spaces"} quotient spaces
              %li
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chen-Ruan+cohomology"} Chen-Ruan cohomology
                  is just the latter rational cohomology but applied after passage to the
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inertia+orbifold"} inertia orbifold
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bredon+cohomology"} Bredon cohomology
                  of the original orbifold, for one specific
                  = succeed "-system." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivariant+cohomology"} equivariant coefficient
            %p
              This suggests, of course, that more of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proper+equivariant+homotopy+theory"} proper
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivariant+cohomology"} equivariant cohomology
              should be brought to bear on a theory of orbifold cohomology. A partial way to achieve this is to prove for a given
              = succeed "-theory" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivariant+cohomology"} equivariant cohomology
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+G-spaces"} topological G-spaces
              to one of the associated
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/global+quotient+orbifolds"} global quotient orbifolds
            %p
              For
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+K-theory"} topological
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivariant+K-theory"} equivariant K-theory
              this is the case, by
            %ul
              %li#PronkScull07
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dorette+Pronk"}> Dorette Pronk
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Laura+Scull"} Laura Scull
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Translation Groupoids and Orbifold Bredon Cohomology
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/0705.3249"} arXiv:0705.3249
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.4153/CJM-2010-024-1"} doi:10.4153/CJM-2010-024-1
            %p
              Therefore it makes sense to
              %em define
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orbifold+K-theory"} orbifold K-theory
              for
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orbifolds"} orbifolds
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒳
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{X}
              which are equivalent to a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/global+quotient+orbifold"} global quotient orbifold
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒳
                    %mo ≃
                    %mo ≺
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mspace{:width => "negativethinmathspace"}
                    %mo ⫽
                    %mspace{:width => "negativethinmathspace"}
                    %mi G
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\mathcal{X} \simeq \prec(X \!\sslash\! G)
              to be the
              = succeed "-" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivariant+K-theory"} equivariant K-theory
              of
              = succeed ":" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi K
                      %mo •
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi 𝒳
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo ≔
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %msubsup
                      %mi K
                      %mi G
                      %mo •
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    K^\bullet(\mathcal{X})
                    \\;\coloneqq\;
                    K_G^\bullet(X)
                    \\,.
            %p This is the approach taken in
            %ul
              %li#AdemRuan01
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alejandro+Adem"}> Alejandro Adem
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yongbin+Ruan"} Yongbin Ruan
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Twisted Orbifold K-Theory
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0107168"} arXiv:math/0107168
            %p
              Exposition and review of traditional orbifold cohomology, with an emphasis on
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chen-Ruan+cohomology"} Chen-Ruan cohomology
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orbifold+K-theory"} orbifold K-theory
            %ul
              %li#ALR07
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alejandro+Adem"}> Alejandro Adem
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Johann+Leida"} Johann Leida
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yongbin+Ruan"} Yongbin Ruan
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Orbifolds and Stringy Topology
                %strong 171
                (2007) (
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9780511543081"}> doi:10.1017/CBO9780511543081
                ,
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.math.colostate.edu/~renzo/teaching/Orbifolds/Ruan.pdf"} pdf
        .revisedby
